# An array element passed to a function's VAR_IN_OUT.
#
# Derivation: a VAR_IN_OUT parameter refers to the caller's variable, which
# the function reads and writes (ed.3 6.6.2.3 "the variable is passed by
# reference", 6.6.1.7.1 table 20; ed.2 2.5.1.3). An element of an array is
# a variable, addressed by its subscript within the declared subrange
# (ed.3 6.4.4.3, 7.3.1; ed.2 2.4.1.2 multi-element variables). arr is the
# program's VAR, initially 0 (ed.3 table 10) and retained between cycles.
# BUMP adds d to the element and returns its new value.
#   cycle 0, i=1: arr[1] = 0+5 = 5, r = 5; arr[3] = 0+1 = 1, s = 1  -> 5 0 1
#   cycle 1, i=3: arr[3] = 1+5 = 6, r = 6; arr[3] = 6+1 = 7, s = 7  -> 5 0 7
#   cycle 2, i=2: arr[2] = 0+5 = 5, r = 5; arr[3] = 7+1 = 8, s = 8  -> 5 5 8
# Each statement has one call, and no other read of arr, so evaluation
# order inside an expression does not matter.
period_ms: 100
program: main
inputs: i
outputs: r s e1 e2 e3
status: conformant
reference: IEC 61131-3 ed.3 6.6.2.3, 6.6.1.7.1 (table 20), 7.3.1
---
0 i=1 => r=5 s=1 e1=5 e2=0 e3=1
1 i=3 => r=6 s=7 e1=5 e2=0 e3=7
2 i=2 => r=5 s=8 e1=5 e2=5 e3=8
